# Organizational root-key custody, council rotation, and recovery ceremonies

## THE CRUX FIRST: secret sharing vs. independent multi-signatures

| System | Mechanism | Numbers |
|---|---|---|
| **DNSSEC root KSK — HSM activation** | Quorum activation of smartcards (physical multi-person control, no key reconstructed) | **3 of 7** Crypto Officers, per facility |
| **DNSSEC root KSK — key backup** | **SECRET SHARING** (Shamir-style; Storage Master Key is *reconstructed* inside the HSM) | **5 of 7** Recovery Key Share Holders |
| **DNSSEC root KSK — signing** | Single RSA key in one HSM. Not threshold. | 1 |
| **TUF / Sigstore root** | **INDEPENDENT MULTI-SIGNATURES.** Each keyholder signs the same `root.json` with their own YubiKey; nothing is reconstructed. | **3 of 5** |
| **Docker Notary v1 / Docker Content Trust** | **Single key**, passphrase-encrypted on disk. No sharing, no threshold. | 1 |
| **Tor consensus** | **INDEPENDENT MULTI-SIGNATURES** over byte-identical bytes | **5 of 9** (n/2+1), evaluated against *the client's own* roster |
| **Certificate Transparency logs** | **Single key**, often a seed file on disk. No HSM required by any policy. | 1 |
| **Public TLS CAs (CA/B BRs)** | Only **dual control** is actually required. §6.2.2 "Private key (n out of m) multi-person control" is **BLANK** in the BRs. | 2 |
| **FROST (RFC 9591)** | True threshold signing — no reconstruction, ever | not deployed in any civic root above |

The distinction is load-bearing and the record separates cleanly: **the only place secret sharing appears in this whole survey is the DNSSEC root's *backup* path.** Every operational signing quorum in production civic infrastructure is independent multi-signatures — and the Tor record shows why (see §5.3).

---

## 1. DNSSEC root KSK (ICANN/IANA)

### Structure (prescribed)
Source: [DPS for the Root Zone KSK Operator, 8th ed., eff. 2025-04-14](https://www.iana.org/dnssec/procedures/ksk-operator/ksk-dps-20250414.html); [ceremony roles](https://www.iana.org/help/key-ceremony-roles); [TCR roles](https://www.iana.org/help/tcr-roles).

- Trusted roles (§4.2.1): System Administrator, Crypto Officer, Recovery Key Share Holder, Safe Security Controller (SSC1/SSC2), Internal Witness, Ceremony Administrator. **A Trusted Person may hold only one role.**
- §4.2.2: a ceremony needs **at least seven trusted persons from four different roles**. Tier 5 = CA + IW. Tier 6 = one of two SSC1 + one of two SSC2. Tier 7 = **3 of 7 Crypto Officers** extract credentials from their individual safe-deposit boxes.
- §5.2.4: KSK private keys are **backed up under a 5-of-7 threshold scheme**; RKSHs are drawn from the Internet community, **organizationally separate from PTI, ICANN, and Verisign**.
- 21 TCRs total: 7 COs East + 7 COs West + 7 RKSHs, plus a pre-vetted backup pool ([ICANN explainer, 2023](https://www.icann.org/en/blogs/details/the-key-to-the-internet-and-key-ceremonies-an-explainer-11-07-2023-en): "around fifty different experts… twenty-one of these divided into three groups of seven").
- COs travel **1–2×/year**; RKSHs travel **every 5 years for validation** and give **annual evidence of safe custody**; background checks every 5 years.
- Two facilities: KMF East (Culpeper, VA) and KMF West (El Segundo, CA). RTO 48h, full recovery at the alternate KMF within one week (§4.5.4).

Complete ceremony list with dates and agendas: <https://www.iana.org/dnssec/ceremonies> — 63 ceremonies, Ceremony 1 on **2010-06-16** (Culpeper, generate KSK-2010), Ceremony 2 on **2010-07-12** (El Segundo, import KSK-2010).

### WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED

**Ceremony 40 — the safe that would not open (Feb 2020).** Primary record, ICANN's own list:
- [Rescheduling Root KSK Ceremony 40](https://lists.icann.org/hyperkitty/list/root-dnssec-announce@icann.org/thread/LE6TSERVR2RBNITQGXNIH6CSEYBUDEXQ/), Kim Davies, **2020-02-12**
- [Update](https://lists.icann.org/hyperkitty/list/root-dnssec-announce@icann.org/thread/HQLQPZ2LFESGOAWXYBXB26GATEDZ6OKT/), **2020-02-15**
- [Ceremony 40 scheduled for 0000 UTC](https://lists.icann.org/hyperkitty/list/root-dnssec-announce@icann.org/thread/Z7KNDGDKD5PNZJ52XHP5Y4W3GIYM23H3/), **2020-02-15**

ICANN's [CSC briefing, 31 Mar 2020](https://www.icann.org/en/system/files/files/csc-root-ksk-management-briefing-overview-31mar20-en.pdf) gives the mechanism and the cost: during pre-ceremony maintenance on **2020-02-11**, *"the device indicated the combination was dialed correctly, but the bolt did not retract to allow safe access."* Recovery took **~20 hours of drilling across two days**, complicated by anti-defeat mechanisms. *"Ceremony was successfully conducted with a 4 day delay."* IANA called it **the first rescheduling in the 10-year history of KSK management** ([The Register, 2020-02-13](https://www.theregister.com/2020/02/13/iana_dnssec_ksk_delay/)). Also: [Schneier, 2020-02-14](https://www.schneier.com/blog/archives/2020/02/dnssec_keysigni.html).

Note the correction to the framing in the brief: **this was Ceremony 40, not 41**, and it was a *safe*, not a safe-deposit box. Note also that **the [Ceremony 40 page](https://www.iana.org/dnssec/ceremonies/40) carries no mention of the failure at all** — the ceremony's public record erases its own incident; only the mailing list and the CSC briefing preserve it.

**Ceremonies 41 and 42 — COVID, and the multi-person control that bent.** [Ceremony 41](https://www.iana.org/dnssec/ceremonies/41), **2020-04-23**, El Segundo: *"a reduced agenda and with minimal in-person participation."* [ICANN's blog](https://www.icann.org/en/blogs/details/conducting-a-key-signing-ceremony-in-the-face-of-covid-19-21-4-2020-en) and [The Register, 2020-04-22](https://www.theregister.com/2020/04/22/iana_key_signing_delay_coronavirus/) give the substance:
- On-site staff cut to **seven**; TCRs participated remotely via YouTube from Mauritius, Spain, Russia, Tanzania, Uruguay and the US East Coast.
- **"The TCRs have wrapped their deposit keys with opaque material, and then transmitted them in tamper-evident bags"** — i.e. the physical keys that constitute the 3-of-7 control were *couriered to ICANN staff*, who acted as substitutes. This is the closest thing in the record to a documented degradation of a civic quorum under stress: the quorum was preserved procedurally and dissolved physically.
- The ceremony signed **nine months** of ZSKs (2020Q3–2021Q1) instead of three. Ceremony 42 (**2021-02-11**) did the same for 2021Q2–Q4. Between April 2020 and October 2021 there were **two ceremonies where there would normally have been six.**

**2017 KSK rollover postponement.** [ICANN announcement, 2017-09-27](https://www.icann.org/en/announcements/details/ksk-rollover-postponed-27-9-2017-en): the 2017-10-11 rollover was postponed after RFC 8145 trust-anchor telemetry showed a significant fraction of validating resolvers were not ready — ICANN estimated **~750 million people, roughly 1 in 4 DNSSEC-validating users**, could have been affected. Causes cited: misconfiguration plus *"a widely-used resolver program had a bug preventing automatic key updates."* Marby: *"We would rather proceed cautiously and reasonably, than continue."* The rollover executed **2018-10-11**; KSK-2010 deletion appears on the ceremony agendas for **Ceremony 37 (2019-05-16)** and **38 (2019-08-14)** — *confidence medium on the exact Oct-2018/Jan-2019 revocation dates; I could not retrieve ICANN's completion announcement (404) and the mailing-list archive for Oct 2018 / Jan 2019 contains only ceremony reminders.*

### **Has the root KSK recovery path ever actually been exercised? YES — once, on 2024-04-25, after 14 years.**

This is the strongest finding in the report. The **[Ceremony 53-1 script](https://data.iana.org/ksk-ceremony/53-1/20240425-KC53-1_Script.pdf)** (Culpeper, Thu 25 April 2024) contains **Act 5: "Test and Replace Recovery Key Share Holders' (RKSHs) Storage Master Key (SMK) Cards."** Verbatim:

> The currently-issued Recovery Key Share Holders' Storage Master Key Cards were generated in 2010, and should be tested for functionality, then replaced due to age. This will be achieved by performing the following steps:
> • Generate a new temporary SMK on the HSM
> • Attempt to import an APP key backup encrypted with the production SMK (with an expected failed result)…
> • Import the production SMK to the HSM using the 2010-era RKSH SMK Cards and successfully import the APP key backup…
> • Generate replacement RKSH SMK cards to replace the existing cards due to age
> • Destroy the 2010-era RKSH SMK cards

Details worth carrying:
- **They ran a negative control first.** Step 5.11 expects the HSM to display `SMK Mismatch` and `Restore failed Error code 120D` against the temporary SMK — proving the test instrument distinguishes success from failure — *before* the positive test.
- Step 5.13: `Restore SMK` using **5 RKSH SMK cards** (`1st…5th RKSH SMK card ___ of 7`).
- Step 5.18 reshares: `"Num Cards?" → 7`, `"Num Req Cards?" → 5`. The 5-of-7 is live and literal.
- **Ceremony rot, measured.** The script's per-credential "Last Verified" column shows RKSH2 (Ondřej Surý), RKSH4 (Jiankang Yao), RKSH5 (Bevil Wooding) and RKSH6 (John Curran) with tamper-evident bags **last opened at KSK Ceremony 1, 2010-06-16** — **13 years 10 months** untouched, against a stated RKSH obligation to *"travel to ceremonies every 5 years for validation."* Only RKSH3 (Ceremony 31, 2017-10-18) and RKSH7 (Ceremony 45, 2022-05-12) had been touched since.
- Because RKSH1's seat was being replaced that day, **only six legacy cards were available and five were required** — one card's margin.
- Published roster (names are public in the script): COs Frederico Neves, Pia Gruvö, Ondrej Filip, Robert Seastrom, Nomsa Mwayenga, Hugo Salgado, Dileepa Lathsara; RKSHs Sebastian Castro, Ondřej Surý, Kristian Ørmen, Jiankang Yao, Bevil Wooding, John Curran, Dave Lawrence; CA David Huberman, IW Andy Newton, auditors Melanie Chen and Grant An (RSM).
- The card PIN is `11223344`, printed openly in the published script. Security is entirely physical custody + quorum; PIN secrecy contributes nothing, and ICANN does not pretend otherwise.

**The mechanism is being retired.** Appendix A of the same script documents both `[14] Keyper HSM Role Cards` and `[15] Thales Luna HSM Role iKeys`, the latter with a `Domain` iKey that *"associates HSMs to facilitate cloning key materials to dedicated Luna backup HSMs."* Ceremony 54 (2024-07-17) imported *"Backup HSMs 1W & 2W containing KSK-2024."* Ceremonies 61 and 62 (2026) destroy HSMs 5E/6E/5W/6W. **The 5-of-7 SMK-card recovery path is being replaced by physical shipment of cloned backup HSMs.**

### Ceremony rot at IANA — the documentary evidence

- [Key ceremony scheduling page](https://www.iana.org/help/key-ceremony-schedule): *"The day following a regularly scheduled ceremony is held as a standby date."* **Dry runs and rehearsals are not mentioned.** IANA's defence against rot is *frequency* (4×/year) plus a standby date plus total public recording — not rehearsal.
- The **[KSK Disaster Recovery and Business Continuity Procedure v3.8, 3 Oct 2025](https://www.iana.org/dnssec/procedures/ksk-operator/KSK_Disaster_Recovery_and_Business_Continuity_Procedure_v3.8.pdf)** contains **zero** occurrences of *test, exercise, drill,* or *rehearse* applied to the plan. Its change log from revision 3.1 (2019) through 3.8 (2025) reads, seven times over, *"Annual review: Update version information and dates."* The DPS §4.5.4 claims plans are *"regularly tested, validated, and updated"* — "regularly" is undefined and the procedure itself imposes no cadence.
- The DR procedure's structure is itself the answer to "what if the council can't be assembled." §4.3.2, *"Recovery Key Share Holders are NOT available"*: the answer is **not** to recover the key. It is to *"Generate a temporary KSK in a new HSM… Publish the temporary KSK trust anchor in the RZ KSK Operator's repository"* and re-establish the shares later. Likewise §4.1.2, COs unavailable: *"drill the safe deposit box if the materials are not accessible using physical keys."* **Every branch of the plan has an escape hatch that abandons the old key and republishes a new trust anchor out-of-band.** The quorum is not a liveness dependency; it is a confidentiality guard with a documented bypass.

---

## 2. TUF, Notary, Sigstore, PEP 458

### TUF spec
[Spec, latest](https://theupdateframework.github.io/specification/latest/): §2.1.1 root keys *"MUST be kept very secure and thus should be kept offline"*; §6.1 rotation = sign a new `root.json` listing updated keys, signed with **both new and old** root keys; all keys except timestamp/mirrors should be offline. If a threshold of root keys is compromised, *"the Root keys should be updated out-of-band."* No secret sharing anywhere — thresholds are counts of independent signatures.

### Sigstore — the healthiest example in the survey
Repo: <https://github.com/sigstore/root-signing>. Live metadata: <https://tuf-repo-cdn.sigstore.dev/>.

- **Initial ceremony 2021-06-18**, conducted entirely in public as a sequence of GitHub PRs — commits `Signing snapshot for lukehinds / bobcallaway / SantiagoTorres / dlorenc / mnm678`. Recording: <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GEuFsc8Zm9U>.
- `1.root.json` (expires 2021-12-18): **all four roles at threshold 3 of 5**, five ECDSA P-256 keys, five signatures. Today's `15.root.json`: root and targets still **3 of 5**; snapshot and timestamp have moved to **threshold 1 online GCP KMS keys**. That relaxation is real and worth naming — two of the four originally-offline roles are now online single keys.
- Keyholders and terms are published in the README: Bob Callaway, Marina Moore, Santiago Torres-Arias (June 2021–), Joshua Lock (July 2022–), Lance Ball (July 2025–); emeritus Luke Hinds (2021–2022), Dan Lorenc (2021–2025). Signing events run 2–3×/year, so **the ceremony is exercised by construction** — it cannot rot.
- v4→v5 (Oct 2022) rotated **all five** root keys at once (hex→PEM keyid format change plus a keyholder change).

**Sigstore's documented failures:**
- **[Issue #422, 2022-09-29](https://github.com/sigstore/root-signing/issues/422)** — the production TUF **timestamp expired**. Root cause: a failed snapshot/timestamp job a week earlier, a 7-day schedule with *"no retries"* between the break and the fix, preprod→prod promotion taking 3 days, and probers that *"weren't running properly"* and *"failed to page."* *"Where we got lucky: Engineer noticed the issue."*
- **[Issue #1431 / postmortem #1435, Feb 2025](https://github.com/sigstore/root-signing/issues/1435)** — root v11 `key_id` mismatch broke third-party TUF clients. Postmortem issue still open.
- **[Issue #1440, "keyholder rotation", opened 2025-02-05, still open](https://github.com/sigstore/root-signing/issues/1440)** — the single cleanest statement of ceremony rot I found anywhere: *"The original intention was always to rotate the root-signing keyholders somewhat regularly **but a real process for that was never setup.**"* Sole comment, kommendorkapten: *"I agree with this, last rotation was around 2 years ago IIRC."* **No activity in 19 months.** One ad-hoc keyholder swap did happen (#1498/#1500, June–July 2025) — ~5 months from the issue being raised to execution.
- Keyholder availability is an explicit written obligation ([SIGNER.md](https://github.com/sigstore/root-signing/blob/main/playbooks/tuf-on-ci/SIGNER.md)): *"if you go on longer travel, please make sure you either have your signing hardware with you or notify the sigstore-keyholders Slack channel beforehand."* Recommendation that new keyholders *"do not have the same employer as other keyholders."*

### Docker Notary v1 / Docker Content Trust — the anti-pattern
[Docker docs](https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/trust/trust_key_mng/): the root key is a **single** key, encrypted with a user-chosen passphrase, optionally on a YubiKey. Docker states it twice: **"The loss of the repository key is recoverable, but the loss of the root key is not."** Recovery means contacting Docker Hub Support to reset the repository. [Notary architecture docs](https://github.com/notaryproject/notary/blob/master/docs/service_architecture.md) confirm: root key *"held by a collection owner,"* timestamp key held by the server. **No threshold, no shares, no council.**

### PEP 458 (PyPI TUF)
[peps.python.org/pep-0458](https://peps.python.org/pep-0458/): status **Accepted**; recommends root as **(t, n) with t > 1**, *"where n is the number of key holders determined by the PSF board"* — **the actual numbers and holders are deferred to the PSF board and are not specified in the PEP.** Keys generated air-gapped, stored on HSMs/YubiHSM or removable media. Implementation deferred until 2019 funding; **full deployment timeline not stated, and I did not confirm that PyPI runs a TUF root today.** PyPI's shipped 2024 work was attestations (PEP 740), which is a different mechanism.

---

## 3. Certificate Transparency

**Custody: single key, single custodian. No secret sharing, no threshold, no HSM requirement anywhere.** Verified against RFC 6962, RFC 9162, static-ct-api, Chrome policy, Apple policy, and the dominant modern implementation. [Sunlight README](https://github.com/FiloSottile/sunlight): *"Secret is the path to a file containing a secret seed from which the log's private keys are derived. This is the most important secret of the log!"* — a **seed file on disk**.

**There is no recovery path, by construction.** RFC 6962 §3.2 makes the log ID `SHA-256` of the DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo, so a new key is definitionally a new log. RFC 9162 §4.13 describes only the orderly exit: freeze, serve `shutdown`, publish a final STH, *"At this point, the log's private key is no longer needed and can be destroyed."* Every observed response is: **freeze → read-only → Retire → stand up a fresh log.** CT survives this because Chrome requires SCTs from **≥2 distinct log operators** (2 SCTs ≤180-day certs, 3 for longer), and `Retired` logs still count for already-issued certs.

**Incidents (all on `groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy`):**

| Date | Log | What |
|---|---|---|
| 2016-04-15 | Certly.IO | Excessive downtime — [thread](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/nyRtXSWSeaM) |
| **May 2016** | **Izenpe** | **Production and test log shared one keypair → identical log IDs.** Found by Graham Edgecombe; a cert submitted to the unmonitored test log got an SCT that verified as production. [Thread](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/g0WWgUodpOk); [retirement](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/qOorKuhL1vA), effective 2016-05-30. **RFC 9162 §4.1 now states: "A log MUST NOT use the same keypair as any other log" — written into the spec because of this.** |
| 2015-11 / 2016-10 | Google **Aviator** | (a) Pilot and Aviator accepted 3 certs with invalid signature chains after a fail-open refactor — [report](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/Itoq0YUZTlA), published only June 2016. (b) MMD exceeded (26.2h peak) — [report](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/ZZf3iryLgCo). Chrome declined to disqualify ([Sleevi, 2016-11-15](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/lsZqdM_u7DI)); Google **froze** it instead, ReadOnly 2016-11-30. |
| 2017-03 | Venafi | Append-only violation: an AWS S3 outage restore from an incomplete backup produced inconsistent STHs, then an STH with **tree size 0** — [thread](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/ohtZ64gLN3I) |
| 2018-02-12 | StartCom, WoSign | Failure to incorporate SCTs within MMD — [WoSign](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/UcCqlxuz_1c) |
| **2020-05-03** | **DigiCert CT2** | **The only actual CT log key compromise.** Signing key taken via the SaltStack vulnerability; the Salt master was publicly exposed because DigitalOcean lacked private networking when CT2 was deployed. Last known-good tree head 17:00:02 2020-05-02. [Thread](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/aKNbZuJzwfM); [retirement](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/f_OpqFEZT9M) effective 2020-05-19. Response: read-only, retire. No rekey exists. |
| 2021-07-21 | DigiCert Yeti2022 | Single-bit flip in a leaf hash; *"Fixing this entry would require generating a SHA-2 preimage… a non-recoverable error for this CT Log"* — [thread](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/hxNohyZncfQ) |
| 2022-09-28 | DigiCert Yeti shards | An SRE ran *"a blanket delete command… to all Cassandra servers of both the new and old clusters."* 2024/2025 recovered; 2023 recovered minus 5 certs → read-only; **2022-2 irrecoverable** — [thread](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/A9uYUOmHM84) |

Pilot/Rocketeer/Icarus/Skydiver retirement (**2022-05-01**) was planned consolidation, **not** failure — [announcement](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/ct-policy/c/abPZR5silrk).

**Negative results:** the "Aviator non-chronological SCT" incident **could not be found** — the two real Aviator misbehaviors are the 2015 invalid-chain acceptance and the 2016 MMD violation; treat the non-chronological claim as unverified. Reason for CNNIC's 2018-09-18 retirement not confirmed. No Nessie-specific key incident found. **No public documentation was found that any major operator uses an HSM for its CT log key — and neither Chrome nor Apple ever asks.**

