## 4. Linux distro archive keys — **STREAM DID NOT REPORT**

The research stream covering Debian keyring practice, the 2008 Debian OpenSSL disaster, the **Fedora August 2008 compromise and full signing-key rollover**, and the **2011 kernel.org compromise** did not return before the deadline. I am not going to fabricate its findings. What I can carry from adjacent sources:

- The **Debian OpenSSL PRNG bug (2008-05-13)** is documented in the Tor stream below and is the same event: predictable keys generated on Debian systems from 2006-09 to 2008-05.
- Everything else in §4 of the brief is **unreported**. Treat as an open gap.

---

## 5. Tor directory authorities

### Structure
- **Identity key offline, signing key online.** [dir-spec](https://spec.torproject.org/dir-spec/outline.html): *"Every authority has a very-secret, long-term 'Authority Identity Key'. This is stored encrypted and/or offline, and is used to sign 'key certificate' documents. Every key certificate contains a medium-term (3-12 months) 'authority signing key'."* Motivation stated plainly: v2 authorities *"used to sign documents using the same private keys they used for their roles as routers. This forced them to keep these extremely sensitive keys in memory unencrypted."* (proposal 103)
- `tor-gencert` manpage: *"this key should be kept offline in a secure location… **DO NOT COPY THE IDENTITY KEY.**"* `-m NUM` default **12 months**.
- **Relay** ed25519: master identity key offline (`OfflineMasterKey 1`), medium-term signing key with `SigningKeyLifetime` **default 30 days**; rotation via `tor --keygen`.
  - ⚠️ **`--keygen-force` does not exist.** Confirmed against `src/app/config/config.c`, `src/app/main/main.c`, and `grep -c "keygen-force"` over the full 41,957-line ChangeLog → **0**. The flags are `--keygen`, `--keygen-family`, `--keygen-onion`, `--newpass`.
- **Quorum: `n_required = n_v3_authorities/2 + 1`** — `networkstatus_check_consensus_signature()`, `src/feature/nodelist/networkstatus.c` line 527. With 9 authorities today, **5**. Consensus method negotiation needs **>2/3** of voting authorities.
- Live roster verified 2026-09-03 (9 voting: moria1, tor26, dizum, gabelmoo, dannenberg, maatuska, longclaw, bastet, faravahar; Serge is the bridge authority with **no `v3ident`** and no vote).
- **Cert lifetimes measured off the wire:** dizum's current certificate runs **18 months** (2026-03-21 → 2027-09-21), **exceeding the spec's stated 3–12 month range**. Nothing enforces the upper bound.

### WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED
- **2008-05-13 — three of six authority *identity* keys compromised** by the Debian OpenSSL PRNG bug (tor26, gabelmoo, moria1). Threshold was 4-of-6; **the margin was one signature.** `V3AuthUseLegacyKey` and consensus method 3 (legacy ID key support) exist because of this. [Dingledine, 2008-05-14](https://blog.torproject.org/debian-openssl-flaw-what-does-it-mean-tor-clients/): *"Tor clients require a majority of signatures before they'll believe the consensus — and that's four of six. (Whew, that was close!)"*
- **2011-03-10** — an experimental IPv6 descriptor *"took down the directory authorities"* (0.2.2.23-alpha changelog).
- **2014-09/11 — turtles dropped for operator abandonment.** [Ticket 13296](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/13296): *"We have a directory authority that's so unmaintained I've given up even reporting problems with it."* Replaced by longclaw.
- **2015-11-18 → 2016-01-07 — dannenberg's identity key rotated and Tor didn't notice for ~7 weeks.** [Ticket 17906](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/17906): *"due to it Dannenberg isn't taking part in the consensus."* The new v3ident was announced by PGP-signed mail to the private `dir-auth@` list; the trust root is a C string in `config.c`. **The network silently ran at 8 signers.**
- **2015-11-24 → 2016-04-14 — moria1's routine annual signing-cert roll broke consensus computation entirely.** [Ticket 17668](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/17668): `Bug: Generated a networkstatus consensus we couldn't parse`, `Couldn't generate any consensus flavors at all!` Roger: *"I wonder if we broke 'moving to a new certificate' somewhere in there?"* **The prescribed rotation path had rotted.**
- **2016-09-01 — Tonga bridge authority retired after ten years, "Tonga's cryptographic keys will be destroyed this week"** ([blog](https://blog.torproject.org/new-bridge-authority/)) — operator Lucky Green stepped down.
- **2020-01 → 2020-05 — authority bandwidth saturation degraded consensus formation.** [Ticket 33018](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/33018): *"We've been having problems establishing a consensus lately… moria1 was unable to serve dirport answers because it was maxed out."* [Ticket 33072](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/33072) still open; source characterized as *"aggressively non-tor sources"*, **not attributed to a named DoS campaign.**
- **2021-01-10 — the January 2021 consensus failure.** [Ticket 40237](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40237): *"the network went several rounds without a consensus… **v3 onion services stopped working for that time period**"* — ordinary circuits survived on the 24-hour "reasonably live" grace window; v3 onion services did not, because they require a *live* consensus. Follow-ons: [40239](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40239) (authorities return 503 during the voting window), [40252](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40252) (retry at :30 if :00 fails).
- **2022-03 → present — moria1's IP blackholed from parts of the internet.** [Ticket](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/network-health/analysis/-/issues/10), still open: *"the entire 128.31.0.39 IP address is blackholed from some parts of the internet… there is a router in the middle of the internet that has a 'drop' rule for this IP address!"*
- **2022-10 → 2024-06 — Faravahar removed and its seat sat empty for 19 months.** [Ticket 40688](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40688): remove *"until its operator can bring it back online outside Team Cymru's network"* (shipped 0.4.7.11, 2022-11-10). [Ticket 40689](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40689) restored it 2024-06-06 with *"Brand new address, brand new keys, brand new day."*
- **2022-11/12 — moria1 rotated both identity keys after a physical break-in.** [Ticket 40722](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40722): *"there was a break-in to the old hardware this month (November 2022) so it is better to do it and be safe."* Commit `72b04a5aa4` says *"there was a known potential compromise"*; **the shipped changelog softened this to "refreshing keys periodically is good practice"** — a disclosure-practice datum worth noting.
- **2022-12-01 — the roster change split the quorum in the wild.** [Ticket 40725](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40725): *"some relays still believe in Faravahar… so if they get a consensus with five signatures including one from Faravahar they accept it… whereas modern Tor clients no longer believe Faravahar is one of the directory authorities, so from their perspective that consensus only has four acceptable signatures."* **Because the threshold is computed against each verifier's own configured roster, one document is simultaneously over- and under-threshold for two populations.**
- **2026-08-21 (open) — [Ticket 41356](https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/41356)**: a rogue authority can imitate another's shared-random commit because `compare_reveal_()` sorts on `hashed_reveal` with no tiebreaker → consensus divergence.

**2014 relay-early attack** ([advisory, 2014-07-30](https://blog.torproject.org/tor-security-advisory-relay-early-traffic-confirmation-attack/)): ~115 relays, ≈6.4% of Guard capacity, active 2014-01-30 → 2014-07-04. **This was a Sybil/relay attack, not a directory-authority key incident** — it is frequently miscategorized. Aftermath: [CMU/SEI, ≥$1M](https://blog.torproject.org/did-fbi-pay-university-attack-tor-users/) (2015-11-11); [Tor's *U.S. v. Farrell* statement](https://blog.torproject.org/statement-tor-project-re-courts-february-23-order-us-v-farrell/) (2016-02-24).

**The argument for independent multi-signatures, stated concretely:** because each signature is separately attributable, Tor could *observe* in 2015 that dannenberg was missing and could reason in 2008 about exactly which three of six keys were weak. A threshold signature would have produced one opaque valid signature in both cases. dir-spec lists threshold signatures only as a possible *future direction* for the shared random value — **never for the consensus.**

**Tor negative results:** no post-mortem documents exist for *any* of these — the record is a ticket plus a changelog line. No "Tor is not being backdoored" 2016 post found (blog URL space enumerated via Wayback CDX for 2015-01→2017-06). Reason for urras's 2016 removal unrecoverable (trac→GitLab migration gap). Reason for tor26's April 2024 key change: none given anywhere. **No Tor directory-authority operator handbook exists publicly** — the procedure lives on the private `dir-auth@` list; the relay offline-key how-to at `community.torproject.org/relay/setup/post-install/offline-keys/` is **404**, leaving the `tor(1)` manpage as the sole authority.

---

## 6. PGP Web of Trust / SKS — the anti-pattern

**Late June 2019 — SKS certificate poisoning.** [rjhansen's gist, "SKS Keyserver Network Under Attack"](https://gist.github.com/rjhansen/67ab921ffb4084c865b3618d6955275f), verbatim:
- *"In the last week of June 2019"*; targets *"Robert J. Hansen and Daniel Kahn Gillmor."*
- *"My public certificate as found on the keyserver network now has just short of **150,000 signatures** on it."*
- *"Any time GnuPG has to deal with such a spammed certificate, GnuPG grinds to a halt."*
- Why it can't be deleted: append-only is *"'baked into' essentially every part of the infrastructure"* — a deliberate cold-war anti-censorship property.
- Why it can't be fixed: *"It's written in an unusual programming language called OCaml"* and there is *"literally no one in the keyserver community who feels qualified to do a serious overhaul."* Recon algorithm attributed to Yaron Minsky.
- Verdict: ***"At present I (speaking only for myself) do not believe the global keyserver network is salvageable."***
- ⚠️ **The word "Hell" for the recon algorithm does not appear in the gist.** Do not attribute it there.

[CVE-2019-13050](https://cve.circl.lu/api/cve/CVE-2019-13050), published 2019-06-29: *"Retrieving data from this network may cause a persistent denial of service, because of a Certificate Spamming Attack."*

**The WoT died as transport, in software, on 2019-07-09.** [GnuPG 2.2.17 announcement](https://lists.gnupg.org/pipermail/gnupg-announce/2019q3/000439.html): **"Ignore all key-signatures received from keyservers"** became the default; new `self-sigs-only` import filter; automatic fallback to `self-sigs-only,import-clean` past size limits; Web Key Directory preferred over keyservers.

**keys.openpgp.org** launched [2019-06-12](https://keys.openpgp.org/about/news) and by design does not carry the graph: third-party signatures are *"a mechanism to attach arbitrary data to anyone's key"* and only holder-attested certifications are distributed ([FAQ](https://keys.openpgp.org/about/faq)). anarcat, [2019-07-30](https://anarc.at/blog/2019-07-30-pgp-flooding-attacks/): *"keys.openpgp.org does not store third-party UID certifications at all, which means it doesn't propagate the web of trust."*

**`pool.sks-keyservers.net` DNS turned off 2021-06-21** — stated reason was **GDPR takedown pressure**, not the poisoning. `sks-keyservers.net` today resolves to `127.0.0.128` (deliberately sinkholed; verified). Dangling defaults persisted: [Debian bug #1002880](https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1002880) for `caff` still pointing at the retired pool.

Prior essays: [Moxie Marlinspike, "GPG And Me", 2015-02-24](https://moxie.org/2015/02/24/gpg-and-me.html) — *"there are only ~50,000 keys in the 'strong set.'"* [Filippo Valsorda, "I'm giving up on PGP", 2016-12-06](https://words.filippo.io/giving-up-on-long-term-pgp/) — *"I never ever ever successfully used the WoT to validate a public key."*

**Negative results:** no dkg blog post on certificate flooding could be found. **No evidence Debian abandoned WoT-based keyring checks** — [keyring.debian.org](https://keyring.debian.org/replacing_keys.html) still reserves the right to demand more signatures *"if it is deemed the key replacement will weaken the Debian web of trust"*; what Debian actually did was move key *distribution* off SKS. Certificate flooding was still a live DebConf24 topic. **No single authoritative "the WoT is dead" declaration exists** — the consensus is inferred.

---

## 7. Root CA key ceremonies

⚠️ **The dedicated CA-incident stream (Bugzilla compliance bugs, lost quorum, ceremony redos, named CAs) did not report before the deadline.** What follows is what I verified myself.

### What the standards actually require — less than people assume
[CA/B Forum TLS Baseline Requirements v2.2.9, 6 Aug 2026](https://github.com/cabforum/servercert/blob/main/docs/BR.md):
- **§6.1.1.1** (root and externally-operated sub-CA keys): CA SHALL *"prepare and follow a Key Generation Script,"* *"have a Qualified Auditor witness the CA Key Pair generation process or record a video of the entire CA Key Pair generation process,"* and obtain an auditor's report. For the CA's own sub-CAs this drops to **SHOULD**. In all cases: generate *"using personnel in Trusted Roles under the principles of multiple person control and split knowledge."*
- **§6.2.2 "Private key (n out of m) multi-person control" is EMPTY.** No stipulation.
- **§5.2.2** is the whole of the actual requirement: *"The CA Private Key SHALL be backed up, stored, and recovered only by personnel in Trusted Roles using, at least, **dual control** in a physically secured environment."*
- **§5.7.3 "Recovery Procedures after Key Compromise" is EMPTY.**
- §6.2.7: FIPS 140-2/3 level 3 or CC EAL4+ modules.

**So: the public TLS ecosystem mandates two people and an auditor's video. It does not mandate Shamir shares, an m-of-n quorum, or any specific n.**

Let's Encrypt is a representative example of what a real CA discloses: [ISRG CP/CPS v6.2](https://letsencrypt.org/documents/isrg-cp-cps-v6.2/) §6.2.2 says only *"security mechanisms which require multiple people performing in Trusted Roles"* — **no n, no m, published nowhere.**

### Rehearsal — the positive case study
[Let's Encrypt, "New Root and Intermediates", 2020-09-17](https://letsencrypt.org/2020/09/17/new-root-and-intermediates/), on the ISRG Root X2 ceremony of **2020-09-03**: ***"Our SREs put together a replica network, complete with Hardware Security Modules, and practiced the ceremony multiple times to ensure it would work flawlessly."*** They also built [`ceremony`](https://github.com/letsencrypt/boulder/tree/main/cmd/ceremony), whose README states the design principle directly: *"unlike most ceremony tooling there is a single user input, a configuration file… The goal is to make ceremonies as simple as possible and allow for simple verification of a single file, instead of verification of a large number of independent commands."* Configs are published at [letsencrypt/ceremony-demos](https://github.com/letsencrypt/ceremony-demos). **This is the only organization in the survey that both automated the ceremony script and documented rehearsing it.**

**Negative results:** **no CA/B Forum or WebTrust dry-run requirement exists.** Every "dry run" claim traceable is vendor best-practice (e.g. encryptionconsulting.com), not a Network and Certificate System Security Requirement or WebTrust criterion. The WebTrust criteria PDF itself returned 403 and could not be read. **No documented case of an HSM quorum card set being lost, or an m-of-n shareholder unreachable at a CA ceremony, was recovered** — this may exist in Bugzilla and simply was not reached.

---

## 8. Ceremony rot

### Confirmed cases where an unrehearsed procedure failed when needed
1. **ICANN Ceremony 40, 2020-02-11.** A rehearsed *cryptographic* ceremony defeated by an unrehearsed *physical* dependency. 20 hours of drilling. See §1.
2. **ICANN RKSH cards, 2010-06-16 → 2024-04-25.** Four of seven recovery shares sat sealed and untested for **13 years 10 months**, against a prescribed 5-year validation cadence. The 2024 test passed — but nobody knew that until 2024. See §1.
3. **Tor moria1 signing-cert rollover, 2015-11-24.** The routine, annual, spec-prescribed rotation broke consensus computation outright. See §5.
4. **Sigstore keyholder rotation.** *"The original intention was always to rotate the root-signing keyholders somewhat regularly but a real process for that was never setup"* — open, unactioned, since Feb 2025. See §2.
5. **GitLab.com, 2017-01-31.** [Postmortem](https://about.gitlab.com/blog/2017/02/10/postmortem-of-database-outage-of-january-31/): five recovery paths, none functional. `pg_dump` ran 9.2 binaries against a 9.6 server and produced nothing; **the failure emails were rejected for missing DMARC signing**, so nobody saw them; WAL archiving off; Azure snapshots not enabled; LVM snapshots were a dev convenience. ~5,000 projects, 5,000 comments, 700 accounts lost; ~25h outage. The sentence that matters: ***"there was no ownership, as a result nobody was responsible for testing this procedure."***
6. **Atlassian, 2022-04-05 → 2022-04-18.** [PIR](https://www.atlassian.com/blog/how-we-build/post-incident-review-april-2022-outage): 883 sites / 775 customers deleted because *"the API used to perform the deletion accepts both site and app identifiers and assumes the input is correct."* The backups were fine — *"no customer lost more than five minutes of data"* — but ***"At the time of the incident, we did not have the ability to select a large set of customer sites and restore all of their inter-connected products from backups."*** Restores took up to **14 days**. **The procedure had only ever been exercised at single-site grain.**
7. **Knight Capital, 2012-08-01.** [SEC 34-70694](https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf): *"Knight did not retest the Power Peg code after moving the cumulative quantity function"* — dead code unexercised for **seven years**; one of eight servers missed the deploy; *"Knight had no written procedures that required such a review."* 4 million executions, ~45 minutes, **$460M**. And in 2011 their DR *test* itself caused a $7.5M loss, remediated by *"adding an item to a disaster recovery checklist."*
8. **Maersk / NotPetya, 2017-06-27.** AD recovery survived only because a single domain controller in Ghana happened to be offline during a power cut and had to be physically carried to London. Recovery root preserved **by luck, not by design.** ([WIRED](https://www.wired.com/story/notpetya-cyberattack-ukraine-russia-code-crashed-the-world/); confidence medium-high.)

### Published rehearsal cadence — what actually exists
- **CA/B Forum BR §5.7.4:** *"The CA SHALL annually test, review, and update these procedures"* (business continuity / DR).
- **CA/B Forum BR §5.7.1, effective 2025-12-01:** CAs MUST have a mass revocation plan and MUST assert *"that they perform annual testing of the mass revocation plan."* Required contents include **"Plan testing - annual operational testing to assess readiness and demonstrate implementation feasibility, using one or more of tabletop exercises, simulations, parallel testing, or controlled test environments that DO NOT involve the revocation of active Subscriber Certificates."** This is the newest and most concrete rehearsal mandate in the survey, and it exists because mass-revocation events kept going badly.
- **Let's Encrypt CP/CPS v6.2 §5.7.1:** *"Incident response plans are reviewed, potentially updated, and tested on at least an annual basis"* and *"performs annual testing of this procedure"* for mass revocation.
- **NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1** (May 2010): cadence is deferred to the organization (SP 800-53 CP-4, *"organization-defined frequency"*). By impact tier: low → tabletop; moderate → functional exercise including *"system recovery from backup media"*; high → *"full-scale functional exercise"* with *"a system failover to the alternate location"*. The only hard annual in the body text is **training**. The "annual" people cite comes from the ISCP template's attestation block. Best line for this purpose: ***"ISCP personnel should be trained to the extent that they are able to execute their respective recovery roles and responsibilities without aid of the actual ISCP document."***
- **NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 Rev. 5 — strong negative.** It covers key recovery extensively (§8.2.2.2, §8.3.1, Appendix B) and **contains no rehearsal or drill cadence at all.** Full-text search for *rehears / drill / dry run* returns nothing. It tells you to *have* a Key Recovery System and Policy; it never tells you to *practice using it.*
- **Casa** publishes **semi-annual health checks** with automated reminders, explicitly against silent key failure ([support.casa.io](https://support.casa.io/knowledge/what-is-a-health-check)) — the only custody provider found with a published cadence. **Unchained Capital: negative** — their Operational Security Guide, grepped in full, has no rehearsal cadence.
- **FFIEC BCM §VII** requires exercises *"at appropriate intervals"*; **no hard annual mandate confirmed in the primary text.** **ISO 22301 §8.5** requires exercises *"at planned intervals"*, no frequency.

**And the biggest negative of all:** the **[ICANN KSK Disaster Recovery and Business Continuity Procedure v3.8](https://www.iana.org/dnssec/procedures/ksk-operator/KSK_Disaster_Recovery_and_Business_Continuity_Procedure_v3.8.pdf)** — the document governing recovery of the DNS root of trust — **contains no testing, exercise, drill, or rehearsal requirement whatsoever**, and its change log since 2019 reads *"Annual review: Update version information and dates"* seven times.

---

## What I could not find (load-bearing negatives)

1. **Topic 4 entirely** (Debian keyring practice, Fedora 2008 rollover, kernel.org 2011) — stream did not report.
2. **Topic 7's incident half** (Bugzilla CA compliance bugs naming ceremony failures, lost quorum, redone root ceremonies) — stream did not report. The standards half above is verified firsthand.
3. **No documented case anywhere in this survey of a civic m-of-n shareholder being permanently unreachable and the quorum actually failing.** The near-misses are: Tor 2008 (one signature of margin), ICANN 2024 (one card of margin), ICANN 2020 COVID (quorum preserved by couriering the physical keys to staff).
4. **No case of a distro losing an archive key unrecoverably** — not searched by me; the stream that owned it did not report.
5. **No CT log has ever recovered from key loss**, because the mechanism does not exist.
6. **FROST / true threshold signatures (RFC 9591, June 2024, Informational, IRTF) are deployed in none of these systems.** Every quorum in production civic infrastructure is either physical multi-person control, Shamir reconstruction (ICANN backup only), or independent countersignatures.
7. `--keygen-force` (Tor), the Aviator non-chronological-SCT incident, a dkg SKS-flooding post, a CABF/WebTrust dry-run requirement, a Thales semi-annual DR-rehearsal statement, and Parity's 513,774 ETH postmortem (**the page has been removed from the web**) — **all searched for, none found.**
8. **QuadrigaCX should not be cited as a lost-key case.** The [OSC review](https://www.osc.ca/quadrigacxreport/) concluded the ~$169M shortfall *"resulted from a fraud committed by Quadriga's co-founder and CEO"* — the assets were largely already gone. It remains a valid *governance* example (no second signer, no tested succession), not a ceremony-rot one.

**Method caveat:** the WebSearch tool's session budget was exhausted at the outset. All discovery ran through `search.brave.com` via WebFetch (rate-limited to 429 partway through), the Hacker News Algolia API, the GitHub API via `gh`, Wayback CDX, and direct primary-source fetches. `web.archive.org` was blocked at the tool level. Google Groups content reached the CT stream via `r.jina.ai`, which returns model-summarized renderings — **CT quotes should be spot-checked against their source URLs before being quoted authoritatively.** Everything in §1 (ICANN) and §2 (Sigstore) was read directly from IANA PDFs, ICANN's mailing list, and live TUF metadata, and is high confidence.
