# COMPLETION: §4 and §7, and corrections

## Corrections to my earlier report

**1. My "the standards require less than you think" framing was half wrong.** The CA/B Forum BRs really are near-empty on m-of-n (§6.2.2/6.2.3/6.2.4 are literally headings with no text; §5.2.2 requires only *dual control*) — verified independently by both of us. **But the m-of-n requirement does exist — it lives in WebTrust, not the BRs.** [WebTrust for CA Principles and Criteria v2.2.2](https://www.lawtrust.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/MDS21216WebTrustCA-222FINAL.pdf) (third-party mirror; whether a later version supersedes it is **unverified**):
- **§3.6.1(d)** — policy must document the *"m of n rule where m represents the number of key shareholders required to perform an operation and n represents the total number of key shares."*
- **§4.4.1** — *"The activation of the CA private signing key is performed using multi-party control (i.e., m of n) with a minimum value of m (e.g., m greater than 2 for Root CAs)."*
- §4.1 requires the ceremony script to carry *"sign-off from participants and witnesses"* and *"notation of any deviations from the key generation ceremony script"* — and parallel scripted-ceremony requirements exist for key **destruction** and **migration**.
- The **Key Generation Report is a standalone WebTrust deliverable**, separate from the annual audit. Let's Encrypt's repository lists one dated 2025-09-30 for the Gen Y ceremony; Mozilla tracks them as their own bug class (e.g. 1850688, 1915531, 2025909).

**2. I overstated "unrehearsed" for ICANN Ceremony 40.** The [KSK Key Management Procedure v3.4 (2021-09-22)](https://www.iana.org/dnssec/procedures/ksk-operator/KSK_Key_Management_Procedure_v3.4.pdf) enumerates unplanned ceremony events explicitly — *"Hardware failures · Software failures · CO credentials failure · Recovery Key Share failure · **Safe failure** · **Safe deposit box failure** · General facility failure."* **The Feb 2020 failure was a pre-written line item, and ICANN's CSC briefing calls the drilling *"one of the worst-case disaster recovery scenarios that had been contemplated."*** The procedure was *anticipated*; what I can still say is that it took 20 hours and had never been executed. That is a weaker and more accurate claim than "unrehearsed."

**3. Same document, §6.8 "Unavailable RKSH Credential Recovery"** — a full prescribed procedure for an unreachable share holder, ending: *"If an RKSH is no longer able to access their recovery key shares, the recovery key shares should be **considered lost**."* So ICANN did think about this; my §1 finding stands that the *shares themselves* went 14 years untested, but not that the scenario was unconsidered.

**4. Ceremony 40 date conflict, unresolved.** IANA's list says 2020-02-16; RIPE Labs says the ceremony ran 2020-02-12; TCR [João Damas' APNIC account](https://blog.apnic.net/2020/03/04/drilling-for-the-ksk/) says the safe opened Saturday evening 2020-02-15. The failure and the drilling are not in dispute; the calendar is. Damas adds the detail that matters most: **three TCRs sat on site for days waiting, because the ceremony could not proceed without them** — and that drilling *"is, in fact, the manufacturer-indicated method."*

**5. Ceremony 41 sharpened:** RIPE Labs (ICANN-sourced) gives the number — **"Four TCRs transferred their credentials to IANA staff to act as surrogates."** <https://labs.ripe.net/author/mirjam/managing-the-trust-anchor-of-the-dns-against-adversity/>

---

## §4 — Linux distro archive keys (now reported)

### The custody reality
**Debian's operational archive signing key is a YubiKey plugged into ftp-master, with the token PIN in a file on the same host.** Verified from dak's production config:
```
SigningHomedir        "/srv/ftp-master.debian.org/s3kr1t/dot-gnupg";
SigningPassphraseFile "/srv/ftp-master.debian.org/s3kr1t/yubikey/pin";
```
<https://salsa.debian.org/ftp-team/dak/-/raw/master/config/debian/dak.conf> — the threat model this defeats is *exfiltration*, not *use by whoever owns the box*.

**Debian's backup IS secret sharing — 3 of 5, and it is the only distro that does this.** From [ftp-master.debian.org/keys.html](https://ftp-master.debian.org/keys.html): *"The program **gfshare** … (a Shamir's secret sharing scheme implementation) is used to produce **5 shares of which 3 are needed** to recover the secret key."* Each share is individually PGP-encrypted to its holder. The five holders are the same five **designated revokers** baked into the key at generation (Burchardt, Jaspert, Faraone, Hymers, Alteholz).

**The asymmetry is the design lesson: destroying trust is 1-of-5, restoring the key is 3-of-5.** Any single revoker can kill the key unilaterally; it takes three to bring one back. The generator script (`dak/scripts/debian/generate-archive-key`) refuses to run unless `GNUPGHOME` is on tmpfs, generates with an empty passphrase, splits, *then* sets the passphrase.

Rotation: *"one ftp-master key per release"* (formerly annual). New keys are signed by two FTP masters **and by the outgoing archive key**, fingerprints confirmed by phone, and announced years before use — bullseye keys [announced 2021-01-28](https://lists.debian.org/debian-devel-announce/2021/01/msg00003.html) for a 2027 expiry; trixie keys [announced 2025-04-06](https://lists.debian.org/debian-devel-announce/2025/04/msg00001.html).

### WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED

**Debian, Nov 2003 — four servers rooted, archive key rolled.** klecker, master, murphy, gluck; entry by **sniffed password** on 2003-11-19 17:00 UTC, then the unpatched `do_brk()` local root; found via kernel oopses. [Announcement](https://chronicles.debian.org/www/News/2003/20031121); [investigation](https://chronicles.debian.org/www/News/2003/20031202). Recovery mail, [2003-12-05](https://lists.debian.org/debian-devel-announce/2003/12/msg00001.html): new archive key `1024D/30B34DD5` created 2003-12-03. **Whether the old private key was actually exposed is never stated** — the roll is documented, the reason is not. Same mail: home directories were "sanitized" by *deleting gpg and ssh secret keys by filename*, with the explicit caveat that anything named otherwise was missed and must be assumed compromised. Separately, the ElGamal sign+encrypt weakness caused *"a bunch of keys"* to be removed from the developer keyring — **count never published**.

**Debian, July 2006 — gluck compromised, NO key impact.** Stolen developer account + fresh local root; *"the only obviously compromised binary was /bin/ping"*; *"neither the regular nor the security archive had a chance to be compromised."* [Report](https://chronicles.debian.org/www/News/2006/20060713). **The 2006 archive key was created 2006-01-03, six months earlier — it was not rolled for this.** This corrects a common conflation.

**Debian, 2008-05-13 — OpenSSL predictable PRNG ([DSA-1571](https://lists.debian.org/debian-security-announce/2008/msg00152.html)).** First vulnerable upload 0.9.8c-1 on **2006-09-17** → ~20 months of key generation. Scope: everything OpenSSL generated, *plus every DSA key ever used on an affected system*. Critically: **GnuPG and GnuTLS keys were unaffected, so the archive key and the developer keyring were untouched.** [The wiki](https://wiki.debian.org/SSLkeys) carries per-service regeneration recipes for 30+ applications. **The official rollover hub `debian.org/security/key-rollover/` is now a 404.** No authoritative total of regenerated keys exists; the "98,301" figure circulating in summaries is **unverified and should not be cited**.

**Fedora + Red Hat, August 2008 — two organizations, one intrusion, opposite decisions.** The [2008-08-22 decision mail](https://web.archive.org/web/20210202162244/https://www.redhat.com/archives/fedora-announce-list/2008-August/msg00012.html) (recovered via Wayback; `listman.redhat.com` no longer resolves): one compromised box *"was a system used for signing Fedora packages"*; ICANN-style split custody saved them — *"the passphrase was not used during the time of the intrusion and is not stored on any of the Fedora servers."* And yet: ***"Because Fedora packages are distributed via multiple third-party mirrors… we have decided to convert to new Fedora signing keys."***

**Fedora rotated on a key it believed was probably safe. Red Hat, facing attackers who had actually signed OpenSSH packages with a real Red Hat key ([RHSA-2008:0855](https://access.redhat.com/errata/RHSA-2008:0855), CVE-2008-3844), did not rotate — it shipped `openssh-blacklist`.** That divergence is the single most instructive pair of decisions in this whole survey.

Fedora's [nine-step recovery plan](https://fedoraproject.org/wiki/New_signing_key) is still live: new key for stable + separate key for testing; new repo location; **re-sign every existing F8/F9 update, testing, and release package**; strip the old repo to a bootstrap minimum; later ship an RPM that purges the old key from users' rpmdb; new key for F10. **The transitional `fedora-release-*.transition` RPMs were signed by the OLD key `4f2a6fd2`** — the compromised-adjacent key was deliberately used one last time to bootstrap trust in its successor. Timeline: Aug 14 discovery → Sept 5 full re-compose → **Sept 18 "recovery efforts are complete."** ~5 weeks, ~3.5 of them with no updates at all. **This is the origin of Fedora's per-release key practice** — `fedora.gpg` today bundles exactly four keys (43, 44, 45, 46). **The promised detailed post-mortem was never published.**

**kernel.org, Aug–Oct 2011 — an N-way developer re-keying, because there is no archive key.** [Hawley's PGP-signed break-in notice](http://pastebin.com/raw/BKcmMd47): trojan on hpa's personal colo machine and on hera, also odin1, precursors on demeter2/zeus1/zeus2; *"Break-in seems to have initially occurred no later than August 12th"*; **modified openssh binaries running live** around Aug 19; root; **vector unknown**. Anvin's [2011-09-30 re-keying instruction](https://lwn.net/Articles/461236/): generate a new 4096-bit RSA key, hold a revocation certificate, sign the new key with the old *only if confident the old was never jeopardized* — otherwise revoke — then collect **in-person signatures from as many kernel developers as possible** (phone signing only if you'd testify in court). A revocation-certificate **escrow was considered and, as far as I can tell, never built.** Site down ~3 weeks, reopened 2011-10-04. **The promised incident report was never delivered, and the promise itself was quietly deleted from kernel.org between 2013-03-01 and 2013-03-02** ([Ars, 2013-09-24](https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2013/09/who-rooted-kernel-org-servers-two-years-ago-how-did-it-happen-and-why/)). Rootkit: Phalanx. Today the [maintainer PGP guide](https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/process/maintainer-pgp-guide.html) states the governing principle: ***"trust must always be placed with developers and never with the code hosting infrastructure."***

### **The answer to "has a distro ever LOST an archive key?" — YES. Kali Linux, April 2025.**

[Kali's own post, 2025-04-28](https://www.kali.org/blog/new-kali-archive-signing-key/), in plain words: ***"We lost access to the signing key of the repository, so we had to create a new one."*** Repository frozen from Friday **2025-04-18** so nobody got a broken update. Every Kali installation worldwide fails `apt update` until the user **manually `wget`s the new keyring**.

- Old key `44C6513A…` created **2012-03-05** — **13 years on one key**, no rotation.
- New key `827C8569…` created 2025-04-17, expires 2028-04-17 (3y).
- They pre-empt the obvious question: *"So your key was compromised and you don't want to admit it, right?" — "No. As you can see we still include the old key in the keyring; if it was compromised we would have removed it and provided a revocation certificate."*
- **The entire cryptographic bridge is: the new key is signed by Kali developers' personal keys, published to keyserver.ubuntu.com.** No secret sharing, no escrow, no revocation certificate for the lost key. The trust transition was manual and out-of-band for every user on earth.
- Precedent at the same project: **in 2018 they let the key expire by accident** — same custody failure, milder symptom.
- [LWN coverage](https://lwn.net/Articles/1019255/).

### Custody model, distro by distro

| System | Signing | Backup / recovery |
|---|---|---|
| **Debian archive** | Single key, **online YubiKey on ftp-master**, PIN in a file beside it | **SECRET SHARING, Shamir 3-of-5** (gfshare). Revocation **1-of-5** |
| **Debian stable release** | 2-of-2 *reinforcement* (archive key + release key) — not a threshold | Not documented |
| **Debian keyring** | Per-developer single keys | No escrow; re-entry needs **≥2 DD signatures** — threshold *trust*, not threshold *signing* |
| **Fedora** | Single key per release, sigul server, **passphrase never stored on any Fedora server** (split by *factor*) | None. 2008 recovery = new key + re-sign the entire archive + one last old-key-signed bootstrap RPM |
| **Red Hat (RHEL)** | Single key | Not published. 2008 response was a **blacklist, not a rotation** |
| **kernel.org** | **No archive key.** Per-developer signatures; signed git tags are the anchor | **The web of trust IS the recovery mechanism** — ≥2 existing-member signatures re-admit a re-keyed developer |
| **Arch** | **m-of-n TRUST, not signing:** one maintainer signature per package; that maintainer key needs **≥3 master-key signatures** | Master key replaced on holder departure |
| **Kali** | Single key, 2012–2025 | **None.** Lost it. Manual `wget` for every user |

**The generalization: nobody does threshold *signing*.** Every archive is signed by one online key. Where m-of-n appears it is in one of two adjacent places — **recovering** a lost secret (Debian 3-of-5) or **admitting** a key to the trust set (Debian 2 DDs, kernel.org 2 account holders, Arch 3 master keys). And every recovery in the record has the same shape: *mint a new key, get humans to sign it out of band, re-sign or re-point the archive, and push the new anchor through the old key if it still works — by hand if it doesn't.*

---

## §7 — Root CA ceremonies gone wrong (now reported)

**Nineteen documented cases. The dominant failure mode is not lost quorum — it is the script and the executed reality diverging, undetected, for months.**

| # | CA | Bug / date | What went wrong |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **IdenTrust** | [1895006](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1895006), 2024-05-03 | **Generated a self-signed ROOT when they meant an intermediate.** *"A PKI operator entered the wrong command parameter"* and *"The ceremony witness missed noticing the invocation of the wrong command until just after the certificate was created."* Correct ICA 5 minutes later; bogus root revoked 2024-05-07. **Fix: split the tool into two separate binaries so the wrong object type is not selectable mid-ceremony.** |
| 2 | **SecureTrust** | [1671037](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1671037), 2020-10-13 | **The ceremony video recorder failed; the recording was unrecoverable.** Their CPS required videotaping with 7-year retention. Because the *evidence* died, **all 7 intermediates were revoked** on 2020-10-08. The ceremony was fine; the proof wasn't, and the keys were thrown away anyway. |
| 3 | **Camerfirma** | [1532333](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1532333), 2019-03-04 | A stale template file carried the **wrong serial number** into an offline CRL ceremony → **revoked the wrong intermediate CA**, disrupting Portuguese electronic payments. *"the configuration file used had the erroneous serial number … and this error wasn't detected."* |
| 4 | **GoDaddy** | [1777128](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1777128), 2022-06-28 | `generalizedTime` instead of `UTCTime` in the ceremony's OpenSSL profile → two non-compliant cross-signed subCAs. Test certs were eyeballed, not linted. **Fix: lint test certificates generated with the identical production ceremony settings, inside the ceremony.** |
| 5 | **GlobalSign** | [2058503](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2058503), 2026-07-28 | subCA created 2025-09-17 with the **EV Code Signing OID instead of the OV TLS OID** (a missing ".10"). Passed *"standard multi-person review"* because reviewers and linters checked CA/B OIDs, not internal CPS OIDs. **~10 months undetected.** |
| 6 | **Let's Encrypt** | [2038351](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2038351), 2026-05-08, **still open** | **Three non-compliant cross-certificates issued as part of the 2025-09-03 "Gen Y" root ceremony.** Reported by a third party **~8 months later**. Stated causes: no linting coverage for cross-certificate rules, *"compliance review fatigue,"* and the rule living in CCADB Policy rather than the BRs. **The CA with the best ceremony tooling in the industry still emitted three defective CA certs.** |
| 7 | **DigiCert** | [1684442](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1684442), 2020-12 | SHA-1 intermediate created in a ceremony, banned since 2016. Linters *"evaluate the individual certificate being signed"* — **not the signer/signee relationship** — so a ceremony can emit a policy-violating cert while every automated check passes. |
| 8 | **Google Trust Services** | [1709223](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1709223), 2021-05-03 | A *remediation* ceremony (2020-08-13) re-signed a GlobalSign root and **kept the SHA-1 signature**, proceeding on *"informal verbal communications with root programs."* |
| 9 | **SSL.com** | [1548720](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1548720), 2019-05-03 | *"Post ceremony actions… including the checks and verification of proper CRL handling were delayed."* CRL published to a dead URL for **~3.5 months**; found by Mozilla, not by SSL.com. |
| 10 | **DigiCert** | [1563573](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1563573), 2019-07 | 22 ICAs undisclosed >7 days. Rowley: *"we don't have a good two-person process to ensure quality control over CCADB listings."* **Fix folded compliance sign-off into the ceremony script itself.** |
| 11 | **SwissSign** | [1506607](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1506607), 2018-11 | *"The organizationIdentifier had been set correctly in the key ceremony protocol (for all 6 IC)"* — but mistyped in the GUI and copy-pasted across five more CAs. Sleevi pressed on whether *"your Key Ceremony was not appropriately followed."* **The canonical script-vs-reality divergence.** ~17 months undetected. |
| 12 | **iTrusChina** | [1923279](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1923279), 2024-10 | **Root key generation ceremony held 2018-07-31 with no Key Generation Ceremony audit report at all.** *"due to our inexperience, we wrongly thought the key generation could be covered by the point-in-time audit report."* **Undetected for six years** — found by the Chrome Root Program on 2024-04-12, not by any auditor. |
| 13 | **DigiCert** | [1613505](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1613505), 2020-02 | WebTrust qualification: *"some HSMs used for backing up offline keys are not operated at FIPS 140-2 Level 3 as per our CPS"* — a firmware upgrade had moved backup HSMs off certified firmware. |
| 14 | **FNMT** | [1626805](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1626805), 2020-04 | AENOR finding: *"Unsafe storage of access codes in PKI Data Centre"* — activation material co-located with the modules, defeating split custody. |
| 15 | **GlobalSign** | [1591005](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1591005), 2020-03-18 / 2021-01-20 | **Deliberate key-destruction ceremonies.** For timestamping CAs they *destroyed the private keys instead of revoking*, because revocation would have invalidated historical signatures. Qualified auditors witnessed and issued **ISAE 3000 reports** attesting genuine destruction. The only well-documented root-program-facing destruction chain found. |

**The Roger Grimes anecdote, sourced.** ["My Key Signing Ceremony Gone Wrong," LinkedIn, 2020-02-17](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/my-key-signing-ceremony-gone-wrong-roger-grimes) (incident ~2015; single-source, unverifiable). Fortune-500 root CA update, C-levels and auditors and legal and both split-knowledge password holders in the room. Boot password fails. Windows password fails. Root cause: ***"a laptop sitting in a safe for five years undisturbed had its 1 key on the keyboard go bad"*** — and both password halves contained a "1". Fixed by moving the disk to another laptop. **The offline root's failure mode was the keyboard.**

**Let's Encrypt's tooling, dated:** [`letsencrypt/ceremony-demos`](https://github.com/letsencrypt/ceremony-demos) was created **2020-08-13**, i.e. *published before* the September 2020 ISRG Root X2 ceremony — the rehearsal artifacts were made public in advance. The 2025 Gen Y ceremony (ISRG Root YE and YR + six intermediates) is [written up here](https://letsencrypt.org/2025/11/24/gen-y-hierarchy.html). **Caveat: the demo configs are public; the production ceremony YAMLs and logs are not.** Their public artifact for a real ceremony is the WebTrust Key Generation Report.

### The decisive finding for the question you're serving

**Every m-of-n scheme in the entire public-CA and root-zone world is SECRET SHARING — Shamir-style reconstruction — not threshold signing.**

- WebTrust §3.6.1(d) defines m-of-n in terms of *"key shareholders"* and *"key shares."* Reconstruction.
- [Entrust nShield](https://nshielddocs.entrust.com/security-world-docs/v13.4.5/edge-ug/security-worlds.html), the dominant CA HSM: recovery keys are *"split among the cards in the ACS using the same K/N mechanism as for an OCS"*; *"Provided that K is less than N … and you have at least K cards available, a hardware security module can re-create all the keys stored on the device."* And the warning that matters: *"We do not recommend creating card sets in which K is equal to N because an error on one card would render the whole card set unusable."* **A hard cliff below K.**
- IANA: 7 shares of one Storage Master Key, reassembled inside an HSM.

**Consequence, stated exactly:** in every one of these systems the key is **materialized in one place at activation time**, and the quorum is a lock on the box, not a distributed signing protocol. ***ICANN's April 2020 workaround — four TCRs mailing their credentials to staff surrogates — is only possible because the shares are transferable bearer secrets. Under threshold signing it would be meaningless.*** That is the sharpest statement of the tradeoff the whole survey produced: secret sharing degrades gracefully under a pandemic precisely because it can be subverted by courier; threshold signing cannot be couriered, and therefore cannot bend.

### Still not found, after direct Bugzilla comment-substring searching

- **No public-CA incident of lost activation shares, a lost HSM quorum card set, or a keyholder unreachable causing an inability to sign.** Searched `"key custodian"`, `"quorum of"`, `"shares of"`, `"ceremony was aborted"`, `"zeroized"` across the whole CA Program product. (In modern CA bugs, "quorum" means **MPIC validation quorum**, not HSM quorum.) **Best read: this class of failure happens — Grimes is one — but it is only *reportable* to root programs if it causes a missed CRL or a misissuance, so it is systematically absent from the public record.** The only real quorum-unavailability event in public PKI is **ICANN Ceremony 41**; the only physical lockout is **Ceremony 40**.
- **No incident of a ceremony run with neither auditor nor video.** Closest are #2 (video destroyed post-hoc) and #12 (no report for six years).
- **Certinomis:** no root-ceremony failure found. **Entrust:** *zero* CA Program bugs whose comments mention "ceremony" — its 2024 distrust was about delayed revocation, not ceremonies. **e-Tugra** ([1801345](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1801345), 2022-11-18, Ian Carroll) was a key-*use* failure, not a ceremony failure.
- **No case of a CA accidentally destroying or bricking a root key.** Only deliberate, audited destructions.
- Whether **WebTrust 2.2.2 is still current** — unverified.

**Method caveat worth carrying:** Bugzilla's `longdesc_type=allwordssubstr` matches each word anywhere in the bug and produces heavy false positives; `longdesc_type=substring` does exact phrase matching and is what every good hit above came from. Across all five streams, WebSearch was exhausted from the first call; the working paths were `search.brave.com` and `r.jina.ai/https://lite.duckduckgo.com/lite/?q=…` via WebFetch, the GitHub and Bugzilla REST APIs, HN Algolia, and **raw `curl` against `web.archive.org` — which works even where WebFetch is blocked from that host, and without which Red Hat's 2008 announcements are simply unreachable, since `listman.redhat.com` no longer resolves.**
